// @ens-test
// @exit 0
// @stdout PASS a pipe with nothing reading it classifies as Closed
// @stdout PASS a call the system cut short classifies as Interrupted
// @stdout PASS a number with more than one cause classifies as Other
// @stdout PASS a number no stream call reports classifies as Other
// @stdout PASS the same number classifies the same way every time
// @stdout PASS the file-system table maps the numbers every library agrees on
// @stdout PASS the file-system table maps this library's own number for a full directory
// @stdout PASS a number that names no file-system condition classifies as Other
// @stdout PASS one number means a stream condition and a file-system kind at once
// @stdout PASS a write answers nothing wrong and the whole count it was given
// @stdout PASS a read answers the bytes a write left, then answers the end of the stream
// @stdout PASS a write through a second stream adds to what the first left
// @stdout PASS a write a stream will not take answers a number and no stream condition
// @stdout PASS a healthy call after a refused one answers nothing wrong
// @stdout 14/14 tests passed
// The stream bridges std.io and std.fs will read and write a handle through, and the two tables
// that classify what one of them reported, exercised on the host running the suite. A number read
// from the wrong place is what these tests exist to catch, since no C library clears its error
// number and two of the three seed hosts cannot be run by hand.

main() -> int {
    return 0;
}
